WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.650
Ask who owns an AI agent's identity
and three offices answer at once.

2
00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:09.038
The way out is to separate the decisions
and give each one a single decider.

3
00:00:09.038 --> 00:00:12.687
AI adds one new executive
and one new kind of decision.

4
00:00:12.687 --> 00:00:15.114
The Chief AI Officer owns the inventory,

5
00:00:15.114 --> 00:00:20.038
high-impact determinations and waivers,
and can't delegate the waiver authority.

6
00:00:20.038 --> 00:00:24.388
The official accountable for the mission
signs the use-case risk acceptance.

7
00:00:24.388 --> 00:00:26.287
Neither authorizes the system.

8
00:00:26.287 --> 00:00:31.413
The authorizing official alone accepts
its residual security and privacy risk,

9
00:00:31.413 --> 00:00:36.538
and the system owner owns the AI system
and decides what its identities may reach.

10
00:00:36.538 --> 00:00:40.119
For the identity itself, the
sponsor, an agency employee,

11
00:00:40.119 --> 00:00:44.012
decides whether the agent should
exist; the technical owner,

12
00:00:44.012 --> 00:00:46.581
who may be a contractor, keeps it running;

13
00:00:46.581 --> 00:00:51.563
and the identity governance officer
decides consent, transfers and orphans.

14
00:00:51.563 --> 00:00:55.920
Two signatures stay two, even
when one person holds both roles.
